        "The story begins at age 13, before there was an online business. The founder sold sweets at school and recalls making around €400 in the best week.",
        "The most revealing part was not the product itself. It was the early instinct to think about:\n- Margin.\n- Profit.\n- Demand.\n- How to increase sales.\n- Whether a small activity could be repeated and expanded.\nThe activity eventually stopped after a teacher challenged it. But it provided an early experience of exchanging something people wanted for money.\n1.1 The basic commercial loop\nEven a small school-selling experiment contains the foundations of a business:\n1. Find something people want.\n2. Obtain or create the product.\n3. Make it available where the buyers are.\n4. Set a price.\n5. Collect payment.\n6. Understand the difference between revenue and cost.\n7. Decide whether the activity is worth repeating.\nThis is a simple model, but it teaches an important habit: pay attention to the economics of an activity, not only the amount of money coming in.\n1.2 Revenue is not profit\nIf a seller collects €400 in sales, that does not mean they earned €400 in profit.",
