        "This stage contains two lessons:\n1. A person may have enough practical experience to help someone one step behind them.\n2. Selling help creates an obligation to deliver honestly and within one's actual competence.\n4.1 The path from content to conversation to sale\nThe sale emerged from a sequence:\n1. Publish content about a topic.\n2. Attract someone with a related goal.\n3. Respond to their questions.\n4. Move into a more detailed conversation.\n5. Understand what they are trying to achieve.\n6. Offer help if there is a genuine fit.\n7. Agree on what the help includes.\n8. Deliver the promised support.\nThe founder's sale was not described as the result of a sophisticated funnel. It came from a direct conversation and a clear interest in learning.\n4.2 A simple coaching offer\nA responsible beginner offer might include:\n- A defined topic.\n- A clear starting level.\n- A limited number of sessions.\n- Practical exercises.\n- Feedback on the learner's work.\n- A realistic description of what the learner should expect.\n- Clear exclusions and refund terms.\nDo not promise earnings, guaranteed clients, or outcomes that depend on factors outside your control.\n4.3 The competence boundary\nBefore teaching a skill, ask:\n- Have I personally done the activity?\n- Can I explain the steps accurately?\n- Can I identify common mistakes?\n- Can I help someone practise?\n- What do I still not know?\n- Would I be comfortable explaining my experience and limitations to a paying customer?\nYou do not need to be the world's leading expert to teach a beginner something useful. You do need to be truthful about your experience and capable of delivering the service you sell.\n4.4 The first-offer worksheet\nQuestion\tAnswer\nWho is this offer for?\t\nWhat specific problem do they have?\t\nWhat have I personally learned that can help?\t\nWhat will I deliver?\t\nWhat is outside the scope?\t\nHow will I know the customer received value?\t\nWhat claims can I substantiate?",
