        "A service business has a different value proposition when it works properly:\n- A customer has a problem.\n- A provider supplies a useful service.\n- The customer pays for the outcome.\n- The provider earns money by delivering value.\nThis does not mean every service business is ethical or profitable. It means the business model can be evaluated through customer value and delivery, rather than relying on asset-price movements.\n7.3 Risk rules for speculative activity\nIf you participate in high-risk financial markets:\n- Never use money needed for rent, food, tuition, debt payments, or essential expenses.\n- Do not borrow to speculate.\n- Understand that the entire amount can be lost.\n- Avoid leverage unless you fully understand the risk—and recognise that it can accelerate losses.\n- Keep records of transactions and tax obligations.\n- Do not confuse a lucky trade with a repeatable skill.\n- Step away if the activity becomes compulsive or starts damaging sleep, finances, relationships, or daily functioning.\nThe transcript mentions a short period of gambling addiction. This is a warning, not a model to imitate. If gambling or trading feels difficult to control, seek appropriate support and put barriers between yourself and the activity."
